WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
Picture a seal swimming in icy water all day with only short hair.

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.700
How does it stay warm?

3
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.740
With four zip bags and vegetable shortening, your child can feel the answer at your

4
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.740
kitchen table.

5
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:21.883
In this thirty-minute activity, your child practices making a guess and checking it

6
00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:27.583
right away. They compare how their hands feel and check whether the result repeats

7
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:28.723
after switching mittens.

8
00:00:30.143 --> 00:00:32.223
Stay with your child from setup through cleanup.

9
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:38.577
Set out four matching gallon-size zip bags, tape, towels, a timer, and a bowl.

10
00:00:39.857 --> 00:00:42.437
Check the vegetable shortening label for food allergies.

11
00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.497
Put about two cups in one bag.

12
00:00:46.777 --> 00:00:48.137
Slip a second bag inside.

13
00:00:49.557 --> 00:00:52.897
Tape the folded rims together and leave the inner bag open.

14
00:00:54.177 --> 00:00:57.497
Spread the shortening about a finger's width around the inner bag.

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.800
Make a plain mitten with the other two bags and nothing between them.

16
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.860
Squeeze out extra air so the fat is the only difference.

17
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.820
Fill the bowl two-thirds with cold water, add two trays of ice, and wait five

18
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:13.820
minutes.

19
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.890
Have your child feel the shortening through the bag.

20
00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:19.790
Ask, 'Does it feel warm or cool?'

21
00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.470
Ask, 'Which hand will feel cold first?'

22
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.030
Write down the guess.

23
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:29.410
Have your child dip both mittens to the wrists, with the openings above water.

24
00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:31.710
Start the timer.

25
00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:36.493
Have your child say which hand feels cold as soon as they notice it.

26
00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:40.033
Record the time when each hand first feels cold.

27
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:45.813
Stop at one minute, anytime your child wants, or at the first sign of discomfort.

28
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:49.053
Dry and gently warm both hands.

29
00:01:50.273 --> 00:01:53.253
Wait about five minutes, until both hands feel normal.

30
00:01:54.493 --> 00:01:55.933
Then swap mittens and repeat.

31
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.930
After the test, ask your child, 'Where did that warmth come from?'

32
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:05.670
Many kids say the shortening made heat, or blocked the cold from getting in.

33
00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.750
Pause for a moment and picture it yourself.

34
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.730
Which way is heat actually moving?

35
00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:20.683
Heat always flows from warm places to cold ones.

36
00:02:22.043 --> 00:02:23.603
Cold never moves into the hand.

37
00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:28.203
Your child's hand made the warmth, and the fat slowed its escape.

38
00:02:29.563 --> 00:02:32.803
A seal has a thick layer of fat under its skin called blubber.

39
00:02:33.943 --> 00:02:35.343
That layer slows heat loss.

40
00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:40.883
Stop for stinging, tingling, numbness, pain, or unusual skin color.

41
00:02:42.343 --> 00:02:44.163
Never leave a child alone with the bowl.

42
00:02:45.523 --> 00:02:46.683
Empty it right afterward.

43
00:02:48.023 --> 00:02:50.583
Keep bags and ice away from children under four.

44
00:02:51.843 --> 00:02:53.363
Call nine one one if choking.

45
00:02:54.683 --> 00:02:55.943
Never use dry ice.

46
00:02:56.917 --> 00:03:02.117
If your child skips cold water, or wants a harder test, try the guide's thermometer

47
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:06.917
version. Then look at a seal together and ask where its mitten is.

48
00:03:08.197 --> 00:03:12.857
Ask what they noticed and how it helps explain why a seal loses heat more slowly.
